st  is from a project in the kasubi tombs in  Uganda this was back in about two  thousand nine we threw a crew captured  the monument and this is the laser scan  data so the Machine sends out these  pulsed beams of light and captures data  points every couple of millimeters on  the structure and what that does is it  allows us to visualize the site like  this but that record is an archived for  the future it's made available to the  public for interpretation but it can  also be used to extract blueprints of  the monument so when just a year after  the work the 3d scanning work had been  performed the site was the victim of  arson and so you know very quickly  within the days following the fire we  were contacted we sent these blueprints  to the site and they were able to begin  reconstruction so the data within our  archive is more than just sort of a  pretty picture it's able to help inform  these restoration efforts and that's why  we got involved in this how we can  proactively documents in these sites so  we use a suite of technology so  everything from the scanner that I  mentioned we use drones to document the  sites and then we also use a close range  scanning to get highly detailed  information of things like mosaics and  frescoes so when we started this project  anka it was all about how we can get  into the the most urgent areas and  earlier this year we were able to take a  substantial step forward with an initial  training of the directorate general of  Integrity's and museums five of their  staff members traveled to beirut we did  two train two days of training at the  unesco offices there unesco is wonderful  in providing space and also doing a lot  of the coordination and then we were  able to do a practical hands-on training  at the ciroc Museum in Beirut so these  these these are employees of the djm  came and over  course of three days were able to master  the technology and the tech went back  with them into Damascus so we did  classroom exercises and we also did the  practical experience and they're now  back the djm has selected a list of  priority monuments working with the eco  most working group this is the first  site that they're working on in Damascus  so this is back in Syria they're doing  this work and then sending back the data  for review so in this case we're we're  getting this building this on the ground  inventory of the structures and we will  have this data for use in any kind of  reconstruction or rehabilitation and  we'll also be able to use it beyond that  in terms of what sex there's a lot more  to do in Syria there are a lot of  discussions now in terms of what will  happen with a site like Paul Mira  especially reconstruction efforts so  there's a lot of work that can be done  in doing this documentation before any  efforts on the ground take place so we  know exactly what exists today and so  we're looking forward to doing that in  collaboration with the djm and then we  want to expand to other countries  project on codes envisioned as this  emergency documentation of at-risk areas  so moving beyond just Syria and Iraq  into other places that are susceptible  but are still accessible is a big urgent  need so we welcome any kind of  collaboration and we also will be  sharing the materials the last point I  will leave in terms of what's next you  know the practical applications for  heritage conservation and management are  really well understood with this data  the thing that's kind of coming next is  really being able to use this digital  data to connect people to these places  and and so this is from an example of a  museum that we work with in Austria  called the ars electronica they're doing  life-sized 3d projections of sites  within our archive as a way to connect  people to these places that you know can  seem really far-flung that this data can  make it real can keep these sites  relevance and  the stories and the end the people  connected so thank you
 

